<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nafpaktos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Naupaktos, Naupactus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nafpaktos, ancient Naupaktos and Venetian Lepanto, is a port town on the northern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, guarded by a Venetian castle and a walled harbour. It served as an Athenian naval base in the Peloponnesian War, was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1407 and taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1499. The naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> of 1571 took its name from the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nafpaktos.jpg" alt="Nafpaktos" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agnieszka_R · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nafpaktos_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Spanish rule Sicily was a bulwark against the Ottomans, supplying galleys and grain for the relief of Malta in 1565 and the <b>Lepanto</b> campaign of 1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its defeat of Charles V in 1541 and its campaigns against Spanish outposts halted Iberian expansion in the central Maghreb, while its ships reinforced the Ottoman navy at <b>Lepanto</b> and in other campaigns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish sea power was shown at <b>Lepanto</b> (1571), where Spanish galleys formed a major part of the Holy League fleet that defeated the Ottomans, but the Armada sent against England in 1588 was defeated by English ships and wrecked by storms on the voyage home round Scotland and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Gjin Bua Shpata took Angelokastron and <b>Lepanto</b> (Naupaktos) in Aetolia, while Peter Losha held the Despotate of Arta.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-angelokastron-and-lepanto">Despotate of Angelokastron and Lepanto</a></li><li><blockquote>An early 5th century BCE bronze tablet records the terms for Locrian colonists at <b>Naupactus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/opuntian-locris">Opuntian Locris</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Naupactus</b> was theirs until Athens gave it to Messenian refugees c. 456 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/ozolian-locris">Ozolian Locris</a></li></ul></section>
